## Proof

**Yes, such a function exists.**

### Construction

**Step 1: Partition $\mathbb{I}$ into $\mathfrak{c}$-many dense sets.**

The additive subgroup $\mathbb{Q}\leq(\mathbb{R},+)$ acts on $\mathbb{R}$ by translation. The cosets $\{x+\mathbb{Q}:x\in\mathbb{R}\}$ partition $\mathbb{R}$ into $\mathfrak{c}$-many cosets (since $|\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Q}|=\mathfrak{c}$). Each coset is dense in $\mathbb{R}$, being a translate of $\mathbb{Q}$. Exactly one coset is $\mathbb{Q}$ itself; every other coset $x+\mathbb{Q}$ (with $x$ irrational) consists entirely of irrationals. These irrational cosets partition $\mathbb{I}$:

$$\mathbb{I}=\bigsqcup_{\alpha<\mathfrak{c}} D_\alpha,$$

where each $D_\alpha$ is countable and dense in $\mathbb{R}$.

**Step 2: Define the function.**

Using the Axiom of Choice, well-order $\mathbb{R}$ as $\{r_\alpha:\alpha<\mathfrak{c}\}$. Define $g:\mathbb{I}\to\mathbb{R}$ by

$$g(x)=r_\alpha \quad\text{when } x\in D_\alpha.$$

Now define $f:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ by

$$f(x)=\begin{cases}\sqrt{2} & \text{if } x\in\mathbb{Q},\\ g(x) & \text{if } x\in\mathbb{I}.\end{cases}$$

### Verification

**(1) $f(\mathbb{R})=\mathbb{R}$ (surjective):**

$$f(\mathbb{I})=g(\mathbb{I})=\{r_\alpha:\alpha<\mathfrak{c}\}=\mathbb{R}.$$

Hence $f(\mathbb{R})=f(\mathbb{Q})\cup f(\mathbb{I})\supseteq f(\mathbb{I})=\mathbb{R}$, so $f(\mathbb{R})=\mathbb{R}$. ✓

**(2) $f$ is everywhere discontinuous:**

Let $x_0\in\mathbb{R}$ and $\delta>0$. The interval $(x_0-\delta,\,x_0+\delta)$ intersects every $D_\alpha$ (each $D_\alpha$ is dense). Therefore:

$$g\bigl((x_0-\delta,\,x_0+\delta)\cap\mathbb{I}\bigr)=\{r_\alpha : D_\alpha\cap(x_0-\delta,\,x_0+\delta)\neq\varnothing\}=\mathbb{R}.$$

So $f$ takes **every** real value in every neighborhood of $x_0$. In particular, $f$ takes values $\neq f(x_0)$ arbitrarily close to $x_0$, so $f$ is discontinuous at $x_0$. Since $x_0$ was arbitrary, $f$ is everywhere discontinuous. ✓

**(3) $\mathbb{Q}\subseteq f(\mathbb{I})$:**

$$f(\mathbb{I})=g(\mathbb{I})=\mathbb{R}\supseteq\mathbb{Q}.$$ ✓

**(4) $f(\mathbb{Q})\subset\mathbb{I}$ (proper subset):**

$$f(\mathbb{Q})=\{\sqrt{2}\}.$$

Since $\sqrt{2}\in\mathbb{I}$ and $\mathbb{I}$ is uncountable, $\{\sqrt{2}\}$ is a proper subset of $\mathbb{I}$. ✓

### Conclusion

All four conditions are satisfied simultaneously by the function $f$ constructed above. The key idea is:

- **Rationals** are sent to a single irrational ($\sqrt{2}$), ensuring $f(\mathbb{Q})\subset\mathbb{I}$.
- **Irrationals** are mapped via $g$, which is surjective onto $\mathbb{R}$ and moreover maps every interval's irrationals onto all of $\mathbb{R}$ (by the density of each piece $D_\alpha$). This guarantees both surjectivity and everywhere discontinuity.

